  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Burnt-orange Bolete (Tylopilus balloui) is a striking fungus in the bolete family characterized by its vibrant burnt-orange to reddish-brown coloring. This species is relatively uncommon but recognizable when encountered in New England forests.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap Color",
      "description": "Distinctive burnt-orange to reddish-brown cap that fades slightly with age",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pore Surface",
      "description": "Yellow to orange pores that may bruise slightly when handled",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Bitter Taste Test",
      "description": "Like many Tylopilus species, extremely bitter to taste (spit out after testing)",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in hardwood and mixed forests of New England, typically fruiting on the forest floor during summer and fall months.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The genus Tylopilus is known as the 'bitter boletes,' and a single taste test confirms this—even a tiny sample creates an intensely bitter sensation that lingers for hours.",
